The [[Walker Art Center]]'s graphic identity from 2005, launched with the Herzog & de Meuron expansion. It was made by the Walker's in-house design studio under [[Andrew Blauvelt]], with Eric Olson of Process Type Foundry. One later source dates it 2006.

It works like a typeface. Instead of bold and italic, though, it comes in groups of related words ("vocabularies") and repeating patterns, which designers combine as each piece needs.

In [[Some Vernacular Design Principles (& their application today)]] it stands in for the Cape Cod house: a fixed set of rules, with additions that become standard once they work.

See also [[Towards Relational Design]], [[Context Is a Place]].

